Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Pitch Buckles Above the Kick
- Story & Context: The debut track begins with one dry gqom kick every two beats and no sub-bass. Mara bows a narrow musical-saw tone above it, gradually bending downward without settling. Thando holds a single low organ pedal that makes the room feel fixed while the saw repeatedly loses its center. Sibusiso introduces sub only after the saw rises back above the low-mid masking zone. Nokwanda enters between two kick clusters with a short bass-trombone pedal that starts as air and hardens into pitch.

  The middle section adds no new harmony. Instead, Mara widens the bend until it crosses a tritone above the organ pedal, while Sibusiso shortens the spaces between kicks. Every time the saw passes through the densest low-mid region, sub disappears completely. The listener begins to hear pitch movement as a cause of rhythmic subtraction.

  In the final sixteen bars, the organ releases. Its nave decay continues while drums stop. Mara completes one final upward bend into near-unison with a residual organ overtone, then Sibusiso gates the room tail to absolute silence before one last isolated kick.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A curved musical saw suspended over pale terrazzo, one black speaker cabinet below it, pipe-organ pedalboard and bass-trombone slide crossing the frame, hard fluorescent maintenance light. No conventional lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “hold the floor,” “bend above it,” “sub leaves here,” and “cut the room.”

Track 2: Organ Pedal Under Missing Backbeats
- Story & Context: Thando begins with the lowest usable organ pedal and holds it through the entire opening section. Sibusiso programs a gqom pattern whose expected backbeat is missing every second cycle. The absence exposes the organ’s mechanical blower noise and the chapel’s low resonance. Mara plays only short saw attacks at first, each one bending upward less than a semitone.

  Nokwanda places muted trombone swells inside the missing-backbeat gaps without turning them into replacement snares. A contact microphone on a steel radiator bracket marks every eighth cycle with a dry metallic crack. Because the organ never changes pitch, the track’s apparent harmonic motion comes entirely from saw glides and trombone inflection.

  Halfway through, Thando changes registration without changing the pedal note, adding a narrow reed stop that makes the same fundamental feel harsher. Sibusiso responds by reducing kicks rather than increasing them. The final section becomes louder acoustically but emptier rhythmically.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Pipe-organ pedalboard in close focus, one foot holding a low note, empty drum-grid spaces projected faintly on a nearby service wall, curved saw and muted bass trombone in soft focus. Spoken fragments limited to “same floor,” “missing backbeat,” “change the air, not the note,” and “leave the gap open.”

Track 3: The Nave Tail Is Cut by Hand
- Story & Context: The EP closer is built around natural decay. Every major sound—kick, trombone, organ cluster, metal strike, saw bow—must be allowed to bloom into the room before the next one enters. Sibusiso therefore uses the slowest effective pattern on the record despite keeping the tempo at 122 BPM. The grid is fast enough to imply club music, but events occupy only fragments of it.

  Mara performs long descending glissandi that end before reaching a stable tonic. Nokwanda answers with breath-heavy trombone attacks that stop before full resonance. Thando introduces the only organ cluster on the EP: a low pedal plus a minor-second reed interval held for less than one bar. The room sustains it far longer than the keys do.

  For the final passage, Sibusiso routes the nave microphones through a manual gate. After a last metal-bracket strike, he closes the gate while the chapel is still resonating, creating an unnaturally abrupt void. No reverb tail remains. Mara then produces one completely dry saw bow scrape close to the microphone. The EP ends before the pitch forms.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty chapel nave seen from floor level, contact microphone taped to a dull steel bracket in the foreground, distant pipe organ and black subwoofer cabinets at the far wall, a musical saw catching one narrow line of fluorescent light. Spoken fragments limited to “let the room answer,” “not yet,” “close it by hand,” and “stop before the note.”

